.\" Manpage for airpods-control.
.TH AIRPODS-CONTROL 1 "August 6, 2026" "airpods-control 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
airpods-control \- control AirPods listening mode and Conversation Awareness
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B airpods-control
.B status
.RB [ --device
.IR name ]
.RB [ --json ]
.RB [ --debug ]
.br
.B airpods-control
.RB [ --device
.IR name ]
.RB ( listening-mode | lm )
.RB ( get | list )
.RB [ --json ]
.RB [ --debug ]
.br
.B airpods-control
.RB [ --device
.IR name ]
.RB ( listening-mode | lm )
.BI "set " mode
.RB [ --json ]
.RB [ --debug ]
.br
.B airpods-control
.RB [ --device
.IR name ]
.RB ( listening-mode | lm )
.B cycle
.RB [ --modes
.IR m1,m2[,...] ]
.RB [ --json ]
.RB [ --debug ]
.br
.B airpods-control
.RB [ --device
.IR name ]
.RB ( conversation-awareness | ca )
.B get
.RB [ --json ]
.RB [ --debug ]
.br
.B airpods-control
.RB [ --device
.IR name ]
.RB ( conversation-awareness | ca )
.BI "set " "on|off"
.RB [ --json ]
.RB [ --debug ]
.br
.B airpods-control
.B support-report
.RB [ --with-write-tests " | " --no-write-tests ]
.RB [ --debug ]
.br
.B airpods-control
.RB [ --version | -v | version ]
.RB [ --help | -h ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B airpods-control
reads and sets the AirPods listening mode and Conversation Awareness by talking
directly to the macOS system audio daemon through a private AVFoundation API
.RB ( AVOutputDevice
in
.BR AVRouting.framework ).
A change takes effect immediately and shows the same on-screen banner as a stem
press. No Control Center, menu bar, or Accessibility permission is involved.
.PP
Reaching the API requires forging one private audio entitlement
.RB ( com.apple.avfoundation.allow-system-wide-context )
inside the
.B airpods-control
process via a small interpose library inserted with
.BR DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES .
See the project README and SECURITY document for the full trust model.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS STATUS
.TP
.B status
Read the current listening mode and Conversation Awareness state. There is no
alias. It excludes battery information, device metadata beyond the name
required to identify each record, and other settings.
Compatibility metadata and consented write tests remain in the separate
.B support-report
command.
.IP
Without
.BR --device ,
print one record for every connected output device accepted by the existing
capability-based compatibility check, including compatible Beats; selection is
not restricted to the AirPods product family. Records preserve the order
supplied by the private macOS system audio-routing context. This is the closest
implementable proxy for the macOS UI order, but the private API provides no
exact or stable UI ordering guarantee. With
.BR --device ,
print only the unique case-insensitive whole-name match; no match or an
ambiguous match produces
.BR no-device .
.IP
Plain output always places each record beneath its device-name heading, even
when only one device is selected. Ordinary printable Unicode is retained;
backslashes, controls, and Unicode line and paragraph separators are escaped so
they cannot change the record layout. JSON retains the original name and uses
normal JSON string escaping. Fields are indented in Listening mode,
Conversation Awareness, Read errors order, omitting inapplicable lines, and
records are separated by one blank line. For example:
.nf
.RS
My AirPods Pro:
  Listening mode: transparency
  Conversation Awareness: on

Studio Beats:
  Listening mode: adaptive
.RE
.fi
.IP
A field is omitted only when the feature is known to be unsupported. An
unresolved listening-mode read is displayed as
.BR unknown ;
an unresolved Conversation Awareness read is displayed as
.BR unsupported .
Both are JSON
.BR null ,
with the canonical field key still present. A genuine read failure retains that
fallback state and adds a
.B Read errors:
line whose errored labels appear in fixed Listening mode, Conversation
Awareness order. In JSON, the record instead gains an
.B errors
object mapping each affected canonical key to
.BR read-error .
.IP
A mixed result succeeds if any selected device produces a usable, unresolved,
or unsupported status result. Only when every selected device produces genuine
read errors and no usable, unresolved, or proven-unsupported field does the
command return
.BR read-error
(exit 5). One field failure does not hide another field or stop later device
reads.
.IP
If no compatible device is connected, or
.B --device
does not identify one unique compatible device, plain output is exactly
.BR "No compatible AirPods or Beats device is connected."
and exit status is 1. JSON is exactly
.BR {\(dqdevices\(dq:[],\(dqerror\(dq:\(dqno-device\(dq,\(dqresult\(dq:\(dqerror\(dq} .
.IP
The command's state sampling is read-only and reads each field once per device.
State reads do not poll, retry, wait for settling, or inspect earbud count or
wear state.
One-earbud operation uses the same getter and per-feature availability behavior
as the individual commands.
.SS LISTENING MODE
.TP
.BR "listening-mode get" ", " "lm get"
Print the current listening mode.
.TP
.BR "listening-mode set " mode ", " "lm set " mode
Set the listening mode. If the mode is already active, prints
.B ok
without issuing a write. Otherwise prints
.B ok
on a verified change, or
.B no-op
if the requested mode is not observed within the bounded verification window.
.TP
.BR "listening-mode list" ", " "lm list"
Print the comma-separated modes supported by the connected device in the
deterministic order documented under
.BR MODES .
.TP
.BR "listening-mode cycle" ", " "lm cycle"
Advance to the next mode in the cycle set, wrapping around, and print the mode
landed on. Modes always cycle in the order documented under
.BR MODES .
The cycle set defaults to every mode the device supports except
.BR off ;
.B --modes
selects an explicit subset of at least two distinct modes. Modes the device
does not support are skipped; if fewer than two remain the command reports
.B unsupported
(exit 4). If the current mode is outside the cycle set (including
.BR off ),
cycle still advances in canonical order from the current mode to the next mode
that is in the set, wrapping around; if the current mode is unknown, cycle
starts at the set's first mode. A change that does not verify reports
.B no-op
(exit 3).
.SS CONVERSATION AWARENESS
.TP
.BR "conversation-awareness get" ", " "ca get"
Print the current Conversation Awareness state as
.B on
or
.BR off .
.TP
.BR "conversation-awareness set " "on|off" ", " "ca set " "on|off"
Set Conversation Awareness. The setter is idempotent and uses the same
.B ok
and
.B no-op
contract as listening mode.
.SS GLOBAL
.TP
.B support-report
Build a local compatibility report from existing device and macOS metadata.
Exactly one compatible output device must be available: the command does not
read device names, does not accept
.BR --device ,
and does not choose arbitrarily among devices. With zero or multiple compatible
devices it prints local instructions and exits 1 before a report, prompt, or
write.
.IP
The read-only report includes only the normalized model identifier, the
advertised listening modes, the advertised Conversation Awareness capability,
whether the listening-mode and Conversation Awareness queries answer, whether
macOS exposes their setters, the macOS version, and the CLI version. The model
name is resolved locally from the Bluetooth product ID embedded in the model
identifier. Advertised modes this CLI version does not recognize are listed
verbatim, capped at six, and filtered through the report's character allowlist.
Missing data is marked unavailable or not reported; the command does not guess
it. The read-only report does not include the setting values returned by the
queries and never invokes a setter. A consented run reads setting values
locally only to plan, verify, and restore its writes.
.IP
The command never reads the customizable device name, firmware version, serial
numbers, Bluetooth/MAC addresses, account data, or raw system dumps and logs. It
never uses the clipboard, sends telemetry, or submits anything. A read-only
report does not change device settings or intentionally interrupt audio;
consented write tests temporarily change the settings in the captured plan.
.IP
Terminal output uses Device, Capabilities, and Write tests sections. Color
distinguishes headings and statuses only when output goes to a terminal;
redirected output is plain, and NO_COLOR or TERM=dumb also disables color. The
GitHub issue field uses the same report data, formatted as Markdown.
.IP
When at least one write test can be planned safely, an interactive run captures
the initial settings and advertised capabilities, displays that plan, and asks
for consent, defaulting to no. The captured plan does not change after it is
disclosed. If a setting changes while consent is pending, that setting is
skipped rather than replaced with a different write. The tests switch through
the advertised listening modes this CLI recognizes and toggle Conversation
Awareness away from the captured initial state and back. They are disruptive:
mode switches are audible and noise control changes while the device is worn.
Do not run them during a call.
.IP
Each attempted write gets its own result row. Accepted but unverified writes
are reported as
.BR no-op ;
setter rejections are reported as
.B setter error
and stop the remaining tests for that setting; a write whose target already
equals the state read immediately before it cannot demonstrate a transition and
is reported as
.B inconclusive
rather than
.BR verified .
After normal completion or a setter error, the command makes one restoration
attempt if needed. An unverified restoration names the final state, gives a
manual-fix hint, and exits 3. An externally delivered SIGHUP, SIGINT, or
SIGTERM caught during the tests stops further writes, attempts restoration
first, and then exits 129, 130, or 143, respectively; an interrupted run does
not offer or print an issue-form URL. SIGKILL, a process crash, or power loss
cannot guarantee restoration.
.IP
.B --with-write-tests
answers the consent question with yes and is the only way to run the tests
noninteractively;
.B --no-write-tests
answers it with no.
.IP
The report is always printed for review before the command asks whether to open
a prefilled GitHub issue form, and that question is asked only when standard
input is interactive. Under a script, pipeline, or CI the command prints the
report and writes the issue form URL to standard error without prompting or
opening a browser, then returns the report outcome: normally 0, or 3 if
consented write tests could not restore the initial settings. The prefilled
Compatibility report field carries the per-mode verdicts but omits the
restoration outcome. If the encoded URL is too long, the command opens the form
without the report field and the contributor pastes the reviewed report in. The
contributor always reviews and submits the issue.
.IP
The CLI has only been verified with AirPods Pro 3.
Other AirPods may work when macOS exposes the same private audio capabilities.
Beats reports are welcome, but Beats support has not been verified. The CLI
reference listed under
.B SEE ALSO
documents the report format, the verdict vocabulary, and the issue-form URL
handling in full.
.TP
.BR --version ", " -v ", " version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR --help ", " -h
Print global or resource-specific usage and exit. Help may appear anywhere in
a command, takes precedence over all operations, and never accesses the device.
.SH MODES
.B off
(Normal/no active mode),
.BR transparency ,
.BR adaptive ,
.BR noise-cancellation .
.PP
For input,
.B trans
aliases
.BR transparency ;
.B automatic
and
.B auto
alias
.BR adaptive ;
and
.B anc
and
.B nc
alias
.BR noise-cancellation .
Output always uses canonical names. There is no alias for
.BR off .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--modes " m1,m2[,...]
For
.BR "listening-mode cycle" :
a comma-separated subset of the modes documented under
.BR MODES ,
at least two distinct entries. Mode aliases are accepted. Duplicates are
ignored. Unknown tokens are
.B bad-args
(exit 2).
.TP
.BI "--device " name
Target a compatible output device using a case-insensitive exact name match.
The option is accepted anywhere in an operational command and may be supplied
once. It never falls back to another device. No match or multiple exact matches
produce
.BR no-device .
.IP
The supplied name must not begin with a hyphen, so an option token cannot be
consumed as a missing value.
.IP
Without this option, listening-mode and Conversation Awareness commands retain
their existing behavior of using the first compatible system output device.
.B status
instead reports every compatible device in preserved system audio-routing
discovery order.
.TP
.B --json
Emit structured JSON instead of plain script-friendly output. Accepted anywhere
in an operational command or global version command. Every JSON response has a
.B result
field. Individual resource operations also contain
.B device
and the command's resulting
.B listeningMode
or
.B conversationAwareness
state; unavailable values are JSON
.BR null .
The listening-mode list command additionally returns
.BR supportedListeningModes .
.IP
.B status
always returns a top-level
.B devices
array, including for a selected singleton. Every record contains
.B device
and only the applicable canonical resource keys
.B listeningMode
and
.BR conversationAwareness ;
resource aliases never become JSON keys. A proven-unsupported field is omitted.
An unresolved field is present as
.BR null .
A genuine read failure adds an
.B errors
object mapping the affected canonical field key to
.BR read-error ;
the object is absent if no genuine read error occurred. If all queryable fields
on all selected devices fail, the top-level response also contains
.B error
set to
.B read-error
and
.B result
set to
.BR error .
.TP
.B --debug
Emit diagnostic logs to stderr without changing stdout, JSON, or the exit code.
Diagnostics cover the entitlement bypass, framework and selector discovery,
device selection, raw state, capability checks, writes, and read-back attempts.
Accepted anywhere in an operational command, a global version command, or
.BR support-report ,
where the diagnostics are the quickest way to see why a device is not
recognized. Because
.B support-report
resolves its device without reading names, no diagnostic line carries the
customizable device name; selection logs
.B name-not-read
instead. Its diagnostics share stderr with the consent and issue-form prompts.
.IP
For
.BR status ,
diagnostics follow each one-pass per-device field read. They do not introduce
retries, reorder devices, or change field presence or error classification.
Unlike the report, the diagnostics are not filtered through the report's
character allowlist, and they can name the installed dylib path, so review them
before pasting them into an issue.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
No supported target was selected, or no unique identifiable report device was
available (no-device).
.TP
.B 2
Missing or malformed arguments (bad-args).
.TP
.B 3
A requested write was not verified within the bounded window, or the
support-report write tests could not restore the initial settings (no-op).
.TP
.B 4
The mode or feature is not available on this device (unsupported).
.TP
.B 5
Every selected status record contains only genuine read errors (read-error).
.TP
.B 129
An externally delivered SIGHUP was caught during the write tests and
restoration was attempted.
.TP
.B 130
An externally delivered SIGINT was caught during the write tests and
restoration was attempted.
.TP
.B 143
An externally delivered SIGTERM was caught during the write tests and
restoration was attempted.
.SH NOTES
Listening-mode writes are checked every 50 milliseconds while the device
settles. Modes other than
.B off
return when the target is observed, within about 800 milliseconds. Changed
.B off
writes use a 1.5-second window because their fallback can bounce between modes.
If
.B off
is not verified, the setter accepted the request, and the device advertises
Transparency, direct writes and explicit cycles into
.B off
report
.B no-op
(exit 3) with
.B listeningMode
set to
.BR transparency .
This is the expected eventual fallback when Off Listening Mode is disabled,
not an observed final sample. With
.BR --debug ,
.B verify.listening_mode.inferred_off_fallback=true
identifies the inference. Rejected writes and devices without Transparency
report the final observed canonical mode, or
.B null
when the final readback is missing or unknown.
.PP
This relies on a private API and targets macOS Tahoe (26); Apple may change it
in any update. The tool probes known shared-system-audio context selector
variants and checks every required selector before invocation. It fails safe
(reporting
.B unsupported
or
.BR no-device )
rather than crashing when selectors are missing.
.PP
.B support-report
accepts no
.B --device
or
.B --json
option. Its options are
.BR --with-write-tests ,
.BR --no-write-tests ,
and
.BR --debug .
The write-test consent prompt is device-specific; report result rows depend on
the captured plan and its outcomes.
